Subject: arm64: dts: exynos7870-on7xelte: keep BUCK1 at or above the boot level

Both CPU clusters run from BUCK1. cpufreq-dt makes a cluster's voltage
request only on its first frequency change, so until then the other
cluster can lower the shared rail under it: observed with the userspace
governor, cluster 1 at 838.5 MHz put BUCK1 at 762.5 mV while cluster 0
still ran the bootloader's 902.2 MHz (OPP 787.5 mV); stepping cluster 1
further down would have gone to 675 mV. With schedutil the same window
exists at every boot.

Set the regulator minimum to 737.5 mV, the BUCK1 voltage the bootloader
leaves at 902.2 MHz (read on this phone). The 546 and 676 MHz OPPs then
run at 737.5 mV instead of 675/718.75 mV: above the table, never below.

Against: linux v7.3.4 + 0001-0014. License: GPL-2.0 (kernel DTS).

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos7870-on7xelte.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos7870-on7xelte.dts
@@ -496,7 +496,13 @@
 		regulators {
 			vdd_buck1: buck1 {
 				regulator-name = "vdd_buck1";
-				regulator-min-microvolt = <500000>;
+				/*
+				 * Not below the bootloader's 902.2 MHz level: until
+				 * a cluster changes frequency it has no voltage
+				 * request on this shared rail, so the other cluster
+				 * stepping down could undervolt it.
+				 */
+				regulator-min-microvolt = <737500>;
 				regulator-max-microvolt = <1300000>;
 				regulator-ramp-delay = <12000>;
 				/*
